    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm updating our HACCP plan for the summer season. Can you give me the exact temperature and time requirements for cooling down cooked poultry? We need to make sure we're strictly preventing Clostridium perfringens growth.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly. To minimize the risk of Clostridium perfringens growth, cooked poultry should be cooled from 135°F to 70°F within 2 hours and then from 70°F to 41°F or below within another 4 hours, using blast chillers or equivalent rapid cooling methods. Adhering to these temperature-time guidelines will ensure that the poultry does not enter the temperature danger zone (between 40°F and 140°F) where C. perfringens can thrive.",
